{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 330 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c4d0c6803f8a6308d669832f23410ee1776aa1da9354b3d2d83c874e91f4bd62" :edb/db-before-t 329 :edb/db-after-t 330 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533642 50 #inst "2026-10-04T05:34:54.741Z" 13194139533642 true] [17592186110593 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243601 13194139533642 true] [17592186110594 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243594 13194139533642 true] [17592186110595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243608 13194139533642 true] [17592186110861 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533642 true] [17592186110861 1145 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/0c779fcd96d5d197e96f954da10b1c30e9eb7fcd189fb37cc215dbb39b912f51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/ba154a9d6283e9e333c484045627c1eca517a8e2771f065256ac4e69c0613126.png)" 13194139533642 true] [17592186111068 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533642 true] [17592186111068 1145 "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/c1bbffed32708d30355ed3592deae845d1a84cd1f1772ef1d0fae7ac39518295.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true." 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243595 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1089 "q-18523" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1100 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}?$" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1113 #uuid "09d1de29-e974-591a-b590-9887d91abf1f" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1138 false 13194139533642 true] [17592186243594 1145 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/4dacecba4626f7c202777cad0a723295a2954f4b0c65fde83a81c6c849414d51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/b747ae8c29f01b840930d81f9c4b706d049ca685ee982efff717ddb13213937d.png)" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243596 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243597 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243598 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243599 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243600 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1041 "selection" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243599 13194139533642 true] [17592186243595 1110 #uuid "149d5b50-9d85-562a-b5b8-116741b28875" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243596 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/1c7ef699169e521845d96c6b28392271c27cca116b449277c66817dc2dbb6491.png" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533642 true] [17592186243596 1111 #uuid "12e6a711-21ab-59ef-b21a-7dab8506bedb" 13194139533642 true] [17592186243597 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931548/assets/dcec9bbf7c5a1badb7e0193a16e8